"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[45251],{445251:e=>{e.exports="# Migrate ETL Project to Spark Declarative Pipeline\n\nConvert an existing ETL project into a Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipeline (SDP).\nUse this skill when the user asks to migrate, port, or convert an ETL project to\nSDP or Lakeflow.\n\nV1 supports **DBT** as the source ETL. The skill is structured so additional sources\n(Snowflake, Informatica, etc.) can be added without rewriting the orchestrator —\nadd a `references/sources/<source>/` pack and a routing entry in\n`references/00-index.md`.\n\n## Inputs\n\nA single argument is required: a path to the ETL source project. This is either a\nWorkspace path (e.g. `/Workspace/Users/.../my_dbt_project/`) or a Unity Catalog Volume\npath. Do not start work without this path — ask the user if they did not provide one.\n\n## Reference files\n\nThis skill is split into source-agnostic phase docs, an SDP target reference, and\nper-source packs. Load each on demand with `readSkillFile`:\n\n| File                                                     | When to load                                                               |\n| -------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `references/00-index.md`                                 | Always, at start — to determine `source_type` and which source pack to use |\n| `references/research-phase.md`                           | Phase 1 — analyzing the project and producing the migration plan           |\n| `references/generate-phase.md`                           | Phase 2 — emitting SDP source files                                        |\n| `references/validate-repair-phase.md`                    | Phase 3 — running dry-run, diagnosing, repairing                           |\n| `references/sdp-syntax.md`                               | Phase 2 / 3 — SDP target syntax (Python + SQL)                             |\n| `references/ir-schema.yml`                               | Research and repair — schema for `migration-plan.yml`                      |\n| `references/sources/<source_type>/overview.md`           | Phase 1 — what's in the project, what files to read                        |\n| `references/sources/<source_type>/decision-rules.md`     | Phase 1 — source materialization → `sdp_type`                              |\n| `references/sources/<source_type>/conversion-rules.md`   | Phase 2 / 3 — source-side syntax conversion                                |\n| `references/sources/<source_type>/examples/<pattern>.md` | Phase 2 — worked examples per common pattern                               |\n\n## Workflow\n\n```\n[ source detect ] → Research → Generate → ┌── Validate (dry-run) ──┐\n                                          │            │           │\n                                          │   clean ───┴──► Done   │\n                                          │   errors ──► Repair ──┘\n                                          └─── loop (max 5) ───────┘\n```\n\n### Step 0: Detect source and gather parameters\n\n1. **Detect `source_type`**. Read `references/00-index.md` and inspect the project\n   root the user provided. If detection is ambiguous, ask the user. V1: only `dbt`\n   is supported — refuse to proceed for unsupported sources and surface the\n   detection table.\n2. **Ask the user** (one round of `askUserQuestions`):\n   - **Output format** — `sql` (default, recommended) or `python`.\n   - **Output directory** — where to write the migration plan and generated SDP\n     files. Default: `<source_project_path>/../migrated_<source_project_name>/`.\n     Confirm with the user.\n\nDo NOT ask the user for a target catalog or schema. The migration writes SDP files\ninto the pipeline and the pipeline's existing catalog/schema apply at runtime. If\nthe user wants a different target, the `editPipelineSettings` call in Phase 2\nsurfaces an approval card where they review and adjust the proposed settings.\n\n### Step 1: Research\n\nRead `references/research-phase.md`. The phase doc tells you to also load the\nsource pack's `overview.md` and `decision-rules.md`. Research produces TWO\nartifacts in the output directory:\n\n1. `migration-plan.yml` — the structured IR (see `references/ir-schema.yml`).\n2. `migration-plan.md` — a human-readable summary the user can review alongside\n   the generated SDP source files.\n\nAfter both files are written, **show the user the summary** (counts by SDP type,\nmedium/low confidence nodes, items needing manual review, source mappings) and\n**wait for explicit approval** before continuing. The user may edit\n`migration-plan.yml` directly between phases.\n\n### Step 2: Generate\n\nRead `references/generate-phase.md`. It tells you to also load the source pack's\n`conversion-rules.md` and `references/sdp-syntax.md`. Read the original source\nfiles plus the IR; emit one SDP file per non-skipped node into the output\ndirectory using `createPipelineFile`.\n\nOnce all node files are written, configure the pipeline:\n\n- Call `editPipelineSettings` to set `serverless = true` and the libraries glob\n  pointing at the generated source directory. The tool surfaces an approval card\n  showing the proposed diff — this is where the user reviews and confirms (or\n  rejects, in which case you adjust and try again based on their feedback).\n- Do NOT explicitly set `catalog` or `schema` unless the user told you to. The\n  pipeline's existing catalog/schema apply by default. If the user wants a\n  different target, they can change it via the approval card flow.\n\n### Step 3: Validate and repair (loop)\n\nRead `references/validate-repair-phase.md`. The loop:\n\n1. `startPipelineDryRun` — run a dry-run of the migration pipeline.\n2. Wait for the update to complete via `readPipelineUpdateStatus`.\n3. Read failures via `readPipelineIssues`.\n4. **Source-not-found errors are expected during migration** — when the migration\n   environment cannot reach the original source data, classify these as expected\n   and do NOT enter repair for them. Report them to the user as informational.\n5. For real errors, follow the diagnosis workflow in `validate-repair-phase.md`.\n   The repair phase decides: code fault (patch the generated file), IR fault\n   (revise `migration-plan.yml` and regenerate the affected nodes), or\n   infrastructure fault (stop and report).\n6. After a repair, jump back to step 1.\n7. Stop after 5 unsuccessful repair iterations and surface everything attempted to\n   the user.\n\nWhen the dry-run is clean, report success to the user with the pipeline link and\nthe list of generated files. The migration is done.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- The Migration IR (`migration-plan.yml`) is the contract between phases. If the\n  user edits it manually, respect their edits and regenerate affected nodes.\n- Never override decisions in the IR during generation. Only the repair phase may\n  change the IR, and only via explicit `ir_amendments`.\n- Do NOT pre-collect catalog or schema from the user in Step 0. The pipeline's\n  existing catalog/schema apply. `editPipelineSettings` is the place where the\n  user reviews and (if needed) overrides those values via the approval card.\n- Always read `references/sdp-syntax.md` and the source pack's\n  `conversion-rules.md` before writing or patching SDP code. Do not improvise SDP\n  syntax.\n- Source-specific knowledge belongs in the source pack, not in this orchestrator\n  or the source-agnostic phase docs. If you find yourself wanting to add\n  source-specific guidance to a phase doc, that's a signal it should live in the\n  pack instead.\n"}}]);
